Ze-Reviewed-Against: github.com/vishvananda/netlink v1.3.1

No upstream pull request is opened yet for these XFRM fixes.

XfrmState gains AFUnspec, which sets XFRM_STATE_AF_UNSPEC in
xfrm_usersa_info.flags and is read back from it. Upstream sets no flag but
ESN, so a tunnel-mode state installed with no selector reaches the kernel's
copy_from_user_state (net/xfrm/xfrm_user.c), where "if (!x->sel.family &&
!(p->flags & XFRM_STATE_AF_UNSPEC)) x->sel.family = p->family;" gives the
selector the outer family, and xfrm_state_look_at then skips the state for a
flow of the other inner family. RFC 4301 Section 5.1.2: "IPsec allows the
IP version of the encapsulating header to be different from that of the
inner header."

XfrmState gains NoPMTUDisc, which sets XFRM_STATE_NOPMTUDISC in
xfrm_usersa_info.flags and is read back from it. Upstream exposes no general
flags field, so a caller cannot ask the kernel to clear the DF bit of a tunnel
header: xfrm4_tunnel_encap_add (net/xfrm/xfrm_output.c) writes
"top_iph->frag_off = (flags & XFRM_STATE_NOPMTUDISC) || small_ipv6 ? 0 :
(XFRM_MODE_SKB_CB(skb)->frag_off & htons(IP_DF));". RFC 4301 Section 8.1:
"This means that it MUST be possible to configure the implementation's
treatment of the DF bit (set, clear, copy from inner header) for each SA."

XfrmState gains ICMP and XfrmPolicy gains ICMP, which set XFRM_STATE_ICMP in
xfrm_usersa_info.flags and XFRM_POLICY_ICMP in xfrm_userpolicy_info.flags and
are read back from them. Upstream sets neither. icmp_rcv (net/ipv4/icmp.c) and
icmpv6_rcv re-check an ICMP error that failed the inbound policy check against
the header it embeds only when the last state of its secpath carries
XFRM_STATE_ICMP, and xfrm_lookup_with_ifid answers -ENOENT to an
XFRM_LOOKUP_ICMP lookup whose policy lacks XFRM_POLICY_ICMP. RFC 4301
Section 6.2: "Thus, an IPsec implementation MUST be configurable to check that
this payload header information is consistent with the SA via which it
arrives."

selFromPolicy sends a non-zero XfrmPolicy.SrcPortMask or DstPortMask as
given, in network byte order. Upstream derives each selector mask from its
port alone, all ones for a non-zero port and zero for port 0, so neither an
exact port 0 nor a mask-aligned block can be asked for. The kernel matches a
selector port as (flow ^ sel) & mask (__xfrm4_selector_match,
net/xfrm/xfrm_policy.c) and reads an ICMP type into the source port and its
code into the destination port (xfrm_flowi_sport, xfrm_flowi_dport,
include/net/xfrm.h), so the
upstream rule cannot express ICMP type 0 (Echo Reply) exactly, nor a code
range as the aligned blocks that cover it. RFC 4301 Section 4.4.1.1: "This
16-bit selector can contain a single type and a range of codes, a single type
and ANY code, and ANY type and ANY code." A zero mask keeps the upstream
derivation, so every caller that sets no mask is unchanged. The reader
already filled both masks from the kernel.

